# id  tag  kind  t_s  alt_km  L_si_W  flag  note
1 - first_light 0.0030 100.000 1.2076e+04 info  L_si first exceeds 1e-6 of its peak. This is just a visibility threshold; the real value depends on your sensor floor and on where the simulation starts (zstart).
2 A ablation_onset 2.7180 85.647 1.5920e+05 stylized  Net heating flux first exceeds radiative loss at T_ablate = 2300 K, so ablation switches on (a kink in the slope). Real onset is gradual over a few km. BELOW this point the model's light is drag work only (no ablated material): treat light above ~86 km as low-confidence. Loosely like the end of Phase 1 / start of Phase 2 in [R22], but the model has no spin, volatile (Na/K) emission or melt physics.
3 Y first_yield 7.2840 62.311 1.4090e+07 physical  Loading rho v^2 reaches S0 = 0.09 MPa. The body starts to flatten (pancake): area, drag and heating all grow, so the light rises faster. Analogue of the onset of fragmentation (Phase 5 / H_f in [R22]).
4 F flare 7.3305 62.079 3.7061e+07 stylized  Light rises x5.6 in 0.17 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 0 at 62.3 km (99% of m0); split gen 0 at 61.9 km (99% of m0). Dust groups supply 46% of the rise and 38% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
5 B first_breakup 7.3620 61.922 6.1943e+07 stylized  Spread factor reached f_split = 2: the body breaks into 4 children plus 20 % of its mass as dust. The dust has a large area-to-mass ratio, decelerates and ablates within a fraction of a second, which makes a sharp flare. The instantaneous release is a model simplification. Light grew x4.4 between first yield and this breakup (pancake spreading).
6 P peak 10.2870 48.086 1.6315e+08 physical  Maximum light. It sits ~+3.8 km from the peak of energy deposition (44.2 km) because the luminous efficiency tau(v) falls as the fragments slow (mass-weighted speed here 17.8 km/s).
7 - drag_overtakes_ablation 12.2670 40.159 8.9453e+07 stylized  Drag work now exceeds the kinetic energy carried off by ablated mass. The ratio ablation/drag is about Lambda v^2/(2 Q Cd), so this happens when the speed falls below ~14 km/s (from the model's heating law).
8 D rolloff 12.7320 38.619 5.9937e+07 physical  Light has fallen to 1/e of the peak 2.45 s after it. Cause: the kinetic energy is being used up (speed 65% of v0, 32% of the mass still in flight) and tau(v) drops with speed. Analogue of Phase 7 (decline until dark flight) in [R22].
9 T tail 16.4730 31.548 1.6295e+05 low-confidence  Below 1e-3 of the peak the light is controlled by groups being retired when they slow below v_dark = 3.0 km/s and by the tau taper (2-4 km/s). Do not interpret structure below this level.
